#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054407 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5bba19b5-e069-5b25-a7e1-00e8058afcd8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Multiplicity of a Root of a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "d7dee7aa-bbb9-50ba-9b0c-6aa0fecbe49d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12994" :question/problem "Which root of the polynomial $P(x)=(x+1)^2(x^2 -x -2)(x-2)(x+3)$ has multiplicity $3?$" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicity of a root of a polynomial equals the exponent of its corresponding factor in the factorization of the polynomial.\n\nFirst, let's completely factor $P(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =(x+1)^{2}({x}^{2}-x-2)(x-2)(x+3) \\\\  & =(x+1)^{2}((x+1)(x-2))(x-2)(x+3) \\\\  & =(x+1)^{2}(x+1)(x-2)(x-2)(x+3) \\\\  & =(x+1)^{3}(x-2)^{2}(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the factored form, we can tell that the roots of $P(x)$ are $x=-1,$ $x=2,$ and $x=-3.$ \n\n- The factor $(x+1)$ has an exponent of $3$ in the factored form of $P(x).$ So the root $x=-1$ has multiplicity $3.$\n\n- The factor $(x-2)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $P(x).$ So the root $x=2$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(x+3)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $P(x).$ So the root $x=-3$ has multiplicity $1$ (and it is a simple root).\n\nTherefore, the root with multiplicity $3$ is $x=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054408 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3a53bf2-3144-5da3-8ee7-54d1e6c8fd7d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using Synthetic Division"}] :question/id #uuid "f83ec339-8cf8-5786-a17e-063842ef9a8b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12995" :question/problem "What is the multiplicity of the root $x=-3$ in the polynomial $p(x) = 2x^3 + 13x^2 + 24x + 9?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=-3$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x+3)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x+3)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x+3)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x+3)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-3$ | $2$ | $13$ | $24$ | $9$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-6$ | $-21$ | $-9$ |\n|  | $2$ | $7$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{p(x)}{x+3} = 2x^2 + 7x + 3\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x) = (x+3) (2x^2 + 7x + 3).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x+3)(2x+1)(x+3) \\\\  & =(x+3)^{2}(2x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x+3)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-3$ has multiplicity $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054409 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054751 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca7f6e86-a083-5dcd-beaa-8c3eae151bf1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using Grouping"}] :question/id #uuid "4600595a-b472-56e5-bce1-e57102880761" :question/math-academy-id "e-12996" :question/problem "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x) = x^3 + 3x^2 - 4x - 12$ have multiplicity $1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. For this polynomial, we can factor by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & ={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-4x-12 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x+3)-4(x+3) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-4)(x+3) \\\\  & =(x-2)(x+2)(x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $(x-2)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=2$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\n- The factor $(x+2)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-2$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\n- The factor $(x+3)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-3$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, the roots $x=2,-2,-3$ all have multiplicity $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054410 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc0ce2c2-36b9-526b-93d6-31bf223ef326" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using the GCF"}] :question/id #uuid "c9da6c44-61e0-5b73-b1b4-fa9e4f0ac440" :question/math-academy-id "e-12997" :question/problem "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x) = x^3-5x^2$ have multiplicity $1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. \n\nFor this polynomial, we can factor by noting that the greatest common factor of $x^3$ and $-5x^2$ is $x^2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & ={x}^{3}-5{x}^{2} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $x^2$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(x-5)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233717 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3a53bf2-3144-5da3-8ee7-54d1e6c8fd7d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using Synthetic Division"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26d49951-743b-5e5a-b56c-f809f185e6fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232430}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26d49951-743b-5e5a-b56c-f809f185e6fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232430} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f86d52e-fde9-552b-a198-2741cd3866ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62cee0e7-d171-51fd-acdf-c0ee91ad3d91" :question/math-academy-id "q-276814" :question/problem "The multiplicity of the root $x=1$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-11x+6$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Since $x=1$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $1$ | $4$ | $-11$ | $6$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $1$ | $5$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $5$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-1}={x}^{2}+5x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-1)({x}^{2}+5x-6).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-1)(x-1)(x+6) \\\\  & =(x+6)(x-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-1)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=1$ has multiplicity $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553523 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc0ce2c2-36b9-526b-93d6-31bf223ef326" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using the GCF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd90c549-a465-53db-bf15-9e662ddaebeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bd90c549-a465-53db-bf15-9e662ddaebeb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553243} :answer-field/id #uuid "75800d24-bf66-5284-95dd-bcb5b2d05b2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "57a69fb9-4cd9-5d72-b269-2597060e6e43" :question/math-academy-id "q-278334" :question/problem "The root of the polynomial $p(x)=8{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}$ with multiplicity $2$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. \n\nFor this polynomial, we can factor by noting that the greatest common factor of $8{x}^{3}$ and $6{x}^{2}$ is $2{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =8{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2} \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}(4x+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $x$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(4x+3)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-\\frac{3}{4}$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5bba19b5-e069-5b25-a7e1-00e8058afcd8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Multiplicity of a Root of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39b569c0-f6ab-5df2-9c28-53eddf4f7979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552984} {:answer/id #uuid "54479ab7-0e1a-56e2-ba26-fe2b95e82f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553050} {:answer/id #uuid "64844d2c-22e0-559b-98b8-36873f7bc01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553083} {:answer/id #uuid "70e83b2c-344b-51c2-88c0-cfd42a83f861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553101} {:answer/id #uuid "c94ec4b3-9bd1-52bc-878d-32cecc37da66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553270}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54479ab7-0e1a-56e2-ba26-fe2b95e82f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553050} :answer-field/id #uuid "90f098a5-6e22-555f-871f-6cf7c6737cc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "472601ac-a114-5add-877d-c16f230ff432" :question/math-academy-id "q-50054" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true with respect to the polynomial $p(x)=(x-8)^{2}(x+5)^{4}(x-4)?$\n\nI. The root $x=8$ of $p(x)$ is a multiple root\n\nII. The root $x=-5$ of $p(x)$ is a simple root\n\nIII. The root $x=4$ of $p(x)$ is a simple root" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicity of a root of a polynomial equals the exponent of its corresponding factor in the factorization of the polynomial. A simple root has a multiplicity of $1,$ while a multiple root has a multiplicity that is greater than $1.$ \n\nFrom the factored form, we can tell that the roots of $p(x)$ are $x=8,$ $x=-5,$ and $x=4.$ \n\n- Statement I is true. The factor $(x-8)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=8$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- Statement II is false. The factor $(x+5)$ has an exponent of $4$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-5$ has multiplicity $4.$\n\n- Statement III is true. The factor $(x-4)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=4$ has multiplicity $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f3a53bf2-3144-5da3-8ee7-54d1e6c8fd7d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using Synthetic Division"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "030a0f2b-a2ac-577b-b389-a20c316bf6b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552870} {:answer/id #uuid "0dc88c27-8fa6-5600-ab8a-26566170f114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552884} {:answer/id #uuid "0eca58f9-55d1-5438-9f0c-b9a382714b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552886} {:answer/id #uuid "95c6d9e5-8395-585e-8861-25ccf04afcef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553172} {:answer/id #uuid "a7afdbe4-6048-5237-a986-11d0fea50f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7afdbe4-6048-5237-a986-11d0fea50f40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553203} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b684267-bcb2-55ea-852a-1abcb22e7f36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553399}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "69d52e3a-df83-5812-9b09-31bab7d15cfc" :question/math-academy-id "q-74152" :question/problem "What is the multiplicity of the root $x=5$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}-x+30?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $x=5$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-5)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-5)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-5)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-5)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $5$ | $1$ | $-6$ | $-1$ | $30$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $5$ | $-5$ | $-30$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-5}={x}^{2}-x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-5)({x}^{2}-x-6).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-5)(x-3)(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-5)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054751 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca7f6e86-a083-5dcd-beaa-8c3eae151bf1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f82ce57-9dc8-512d-ba56-c12b4ef581eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\pm 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552927} {:answer/id #uuid "7eddaf4d-9b66-56d0-9ee9-466e740f3712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553123} {:answer/id #uuid "c2264060-92fe-5dcb-bb3b-b50ba0ef729c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553252} {:answer/id #uuid "e1563952-be3d-5a19-858f-b3ebaf72d7d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553317} {:answer/id #uuid "f54223bc-c0e7-5355-879b-be850f9bedd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f54223bc-c0e7-5355-879b-be850f9bedd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553351} :answer-field/id #uuid "440e0801-7310-5a2d-b0f3-200501423f5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553405}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc7229f0-8eb0-5dab-80da-c569facaf181" :question/math-academy-id "q-74161" :question/problem "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+32x+16$ have multiplicity $2?$" :question/worked-solution "We factor the cubic by grouping, and simplify until the polynomial is fully factored:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =2{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+32x+16 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(2x+1)+16(2x+1) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+16)(2x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the factor $({x}^{2}+16)$ cannot be factored any further, and it does not have any real roots.\n\nThe only real root of $p(x)$ comes from the factor $(2x+1).$ This factor has a root of $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ and the exponent of the factor is $1.$ Therefore, the root $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, there is no root with multiplicity $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553589 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5bba19b5-e069-5b25-a7e1-00e8058afcd8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Multiplicity of a Root of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f02a9a3-6153-5c63-bd62-10b00f44a18b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552996} {:answer/id #uuid "4607988e-13e0-57c7-91f2-d9f4ea3cddad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553011} {:answer/id #uuid "90ff9b37-dfc1-5b29-9094-da3f27f9363e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553158} {:answer/id #uuid "9423ad4d-888c-50c6-97af-2e2a7ced099c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553169} {:answer/id #uuid "e4050deb-3d6a-55bd-92c1-7d62a182eb05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553322}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4607988e-13e0-57c7-91f2-d9f4ea3cddad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553011} :answer-field/id #uuid "980f45e9-ae94-5825-9cc5-feec3bc58c31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f56f30e8-2f9d-5691-bca5-63c90f4a552c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74229" :question/problem "Which root(s) of the polynomial $P(x)=(x+3)({x}^{2}-4)^{3}$ have multiplicity $3?$" :question/worked-solution "The multiplicity of a root of a polynomial equals the exponent of its corresponding factor in the factorization of the polynomial.\n\nFirst, let's factor $P(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =(x+3)({x}^{2}-4)^{3} \\\\  & =(x+3)((x+2)(x-2))^{3} \\\\  & =(x+3)(x+2)^{3}(x-2)^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $(x+3)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $P(x).$ So the root $x=-3$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\n- The factor $(x+2)$ has an exponent of $3$ in the factored form of $P(x).$ So the root $x=-2$ has multiplicity $3.$\n\n- The factor $(x-2)$ has an exponent of $3$ in the factored form of $P(x).$ So the root $x=2$ has multiplicity $3.$\n\nTherefore, the roots with multiplicity $3$ are $x=-2$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553565 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc0ce2c2-36b9-526b-93d6-31bf223ef326" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using the GCF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "262906c0-ef10-576f-8199-e244780a28a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552934} {:answer/id #uuid "36ab4b5b-8a2a-5b0e-8724-8b79f8f8d7ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0,\\,\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552974} {:answer/id #uuid "49588432-8d35-593b-be31-d4dafbefd809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553022} {:answer/id #uuid "6d1477f4-30b5-570a-ad08-73cacb76e00d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553091} {:answer/id #uuid "cdabc4a5-58fc-52df-8905-31d909b6109b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{4},\\,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553281}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "262906c0-ef10-576f-8199-e244780a28a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552934} :answer-field/id #uuid "c63b3ecc-2e05-511b-8089-51402be17be5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b274acdf-9a74-5462-a5c4-3ce49892bee1" :question/math-academy-id "q-74273" :question/problem "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=16{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}$ have multiplicity $1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. \n\nFor this polynomial, we can factor by noting that the greatest common factor of $16{x}^{3}$ and $-4{x}^{2}$ is $4{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =16{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2} \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}(4x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor ${x}^{2}$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(4x-1)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ has multiplicity $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186553506 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054751 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca7f6e86-a083-5dcd-beaa-8c3eae151bf1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "461612fc-907b-5aa8-8c1d-491abe6d8231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553012} {:answer/id #uuid "602e1e9e-c48b-5826-889b-ecc728635cf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553074} {:answer/id #uuid "6c8aee5a-344c-5527-bd91-a64648742232" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553089} {:answer/id #uuid "89723199-da84-5834-8fd3-12a77b90620e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,-\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553143} {:answer/id #uuid "92dbd0f5-bd1d-5d4e-b8ea-886922cda0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89723199-da84-5834-8fd3-12a77b90620e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1,\\,-\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553143} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbae0ca6-9a6a-501c-a55e-8ce32043c2fe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553469}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "269f2437-4ee3-5068-a652-f35cac84deec" :question/math-academy-id "q-74278" :question/problem "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=9{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}-x-1$ have multiplicity $1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. For this polynomial, we can factor by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =9{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}-x-1 \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}(x+1)-1(x+1) \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-1)(x+1) \\\\  & =(3x+1)(3x-1)(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $(3x+1)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\n- The factor $(3x-1)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\n- The factor $(x+1)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-1$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, the roots $x=-1,\\,-\\frac{1}{3},\\,\\frac{1}{3}$ all have multiplicity $1.$"}]}
